  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mst Shameema Begum vs Javid Iqbal Khan on 21 August, 2024 Author: Sanjay Dhar Bench: Sanjay Dhar Sr. No.49 Regular List High Court Of Jammu & Kashmir And Ladakh At Srinagar Crm(M) No.36/2023 Mst Shameema Begum ... Petitioner(S) Through: - Mr. Sheikh Manzoor, Advocate. Vs. Javid Iqbal Khan ...Respondent(S) Through: - None. Coram: Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Sanjay Dhar, Judge Order",
    "facts": "(ORAL) 21.08.2024 1) The petitioner has challenged order dated 14.12.2022 passed by learned Principal Sessions Judge, Kupwara, whereby the appeal filed by the respondent against order dated 07.12.2021 passed by learned Judicial Magistrate 1st Class (Munsiff), in a complaint filed by the petitioner under Section 12 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act (hereinafter referred to as \"the DV Act\"), has been allowed and the order of the trial Magistrate has been [LEAKAGE_MASK]. 2) Notice of the petition was served upon the respondent but despite service, he did not choose to appear and contest the case. 3) I have heard learned counsel for the petitioner and perused record of the case. 4) It appears that the petitioner, who happens to be the wife of the respondent, filed a petition under Section 12 of the DV Act against her husband (respondent herein) before the Court of Judicial Magistrate 1 st Page |2 Class (Munsiff), Kupwara (hereinafter referred to as \"the trial Magistrate\"). She sought monetary compensation as well as the order relating to residence from the respondent. Vide the said order, the learned trial Magistrate declined the relief of interim monetary compensation to the petitioner on the ground that she is working Page |3 as a Government Teacher and has sufficient income. Vide impugned order dated 14.12.2022, the learned Sessions Judge allowed the appeal and [LEAKAGE_MASK] the order of the trial Magistrate, presumably, on the ground that the relief relating to residence to an aggrieved person can be granted only after trial while disposing of an application under Section 12 of the DV Act finally.",
    "arguments": "9) The learned counsel appearing on behalf of the petitioner has assailed the impugned order by contending that the learned Appellate Court has misconstrued the provisions contained in Sections 19 and 23 of the DV Act, as a result of which grave miscarriage has been committed by the said Court while passing the impugned order."
  },
